hi everyone  this is professor m science and today i  want to discuss measurements in quantum  mechanics  with an emphasis on the mathematical  details  this is another one of our videos on  rigorous quantum mechanics  in the companion video link in the  description we explore the conceptual  understanding of measurements in quantum  mechanics  in that video we find it's impossible to  predict the precise outcome of a quantum  measurement all we can predict  is the likelihood of a given outcome  today we start with that conceptual  understanding  and then we expand it to explore the  mathematical subtleties associated with  for example  the generate eigenvalues or a continuous  rather than a discrete spectrum of  eigenvalues  let's go consider a physical quantity a  that in quantum mechanics is represented  by a hermitian operator a  called an observable we call the  observable  a to keep the discussion general but for  concreteness you can think of this as  say the energy or the position of a  particle  this observable a obeys an eigenvalue  equation as shown here  where lambda n are the eigenvalues and u  n the eigenstates  in the companion video on measurements  we introduce postulate 4  that tells us that when we measure a in  a system in state  psi then the outcome of that measurement  is an eigenvalue lambda n  of the operator a and i will get that  result with probability  given by this expression  we can find an alternative form for this  probability if we write the state psi  in the basis of eigenstates u n of the  operator a as usual  and the expansion coefficients c are  given by the projection of psi  onto the u basis states this means that  we can also write the probability  p of measuring eigenvalue lambda n as  absolute value of c  n squared in the companion video on  measurements we go into some detail  about the conceptual understanding of  this postulate so if you haven't seen  that video yet i recommend that you do  that first  and come back to this video afterwards  what we want to do in this video  is to unpack some of the mathematical  details of postulate4  they don't affect the conceptual  understanding of the postulate but are  useful for calculations  the main point is that when writing  postulate fall we're implicitly making a  number of assumptions  the first is that the state psi is  normalized  the second is that we're assuming that  the eigenvalues form a non-degenerate  spectrum  and the third is that the eigenvalues  form a discrete spectrum  what we'll do in this video is to  investigate what happens when we relax  these assumptions  and how we then have to update postulate  4 accordingly  let's start with normalization postulate  4  tells us the probability p associated  with every possible outcome of the  measurement  however when we measure a we will get  some outcome  so this means that the sum over all  probabilities should add to 1.  so is this the case let's calculate the  norm  psi psi of the state of our system we  can then write both bra and ket  in the u basis as usual  we can move the sum over m to the  beginning and we rearrange the  expression to get this  the bracket here is delta n m because  the u stays from an orthonormal basis  and this means that we can easily  perform the sum over m and we end up  with a single sum over  n of the absolute value squared of cn  looking at the probability up here from  postulate 4  we see that this is simply equal to sum  over n of the probability of measuring  eigenvalue lambda n  so what does this mean if the state psi  is normalized then we correctly get that  the sum over the probabilities of all  possible outcomes gives one  however if psi is not normalized but  instead this bracket is equal to some  other value  n then the probabilities as written in  postulate 4  don't add up to 1. to ensure that they  do add up to 1  we need to modify the probability and  rescale it by the normalization n  we can also replace the norm by the  bracket of psi with itself  and we get this final expression  so this is telling us that we can  generalize postulate 4  to the case where the state side of the  system is not normalized  if we use this expression here  with this more general expression we  have just the right for the probability  of measuring lambda n  we can now clarify a point that we first  made in the video on eigenvalues and  eigenstates  let our system be in state psi then the  probability of measuring lambda n when  our system is in state psi is given by  this expression we just derived  in this expression i have written this  sub index here to emphasize the fact  that the state of the system  is psi now let's construct a new ket psi  prime equal to r e to the power i theta  times psi  where r is a real number psi prime is  related to psi by changing its length by  a factor r  and its phase by an angle theta the  argument we made in the video on  eigenvalues in diagonal states  was that quantum mechanics is not  affected by such changes  and that psi and psi prime lead to the  same physics  we're now in a position to confirm this  by checking that the outcome of a  measurement  is the same whether we use the state psi  or the state  psi prime the probability of obtaining  eigenvalue lambda n  when the system is in state psi prime is  given by this expression  and again i emphasize that this  corresponds to the system in state  psi prime with this sub-index  now let's rewrite psi prime in terms of  psi and in doing this remember that we  need to take the complex conjugate when  we were with brass in dual space  in the denominator this exponential  cancels with this exponential  so this term is r squared in the  numerator  the absolute value squared of these two  terms is simply r squared  this means that this r squared cancels  with this other r squared  and overall we get this expression  comparing this result with this result  we see that the probability of measuring  lambda n when the system is in state  psi is equal to the probability of  measuring lambda n  when the system is in state psi prime  this confirms that predictions in  quantum mechanics are  independent of a scaling and the phase  change in our state  we next want to look at what happens  when we have degenerate  eigenvalues if lambda n is a degenerate  eigenvalue  then the eigenvalue equation at the top  here needs to be updated  to a acting on u and i equals lambda n  u n i where i runs from 1 to g n  we have already encountered this  eigenvalue equation for the general  eigenvalues lambda n in the video on  eigenvalues and eigenstates  it is telling us that there are gn  eigenstates that share the same  eigenvalue  and these g and different eigenstates  are labeled by the index i  you'll remember that the eigenstates  associated with the degenerate  eigenvalue lambda n  span a g n dimensional subspace of state  space  and we can always make linear  combinations of these eigen states to  get equally valid but alternative bases  exploiting this fact we can always  choose these eigenstates to form an  orthonormal basis  and we can therefore expand the state  psi of our system in the basis of  eigenstates of a  in this expansion i have explicitly  separated the sum into two terms  one over the distinct eigenvalues  labeled by n  and then the other over i taking into  account the degeneracies of a given  eigenvalue  this also means that the expansion  coefficients c now have an extra i index  and this of course follows from the fact  that the expansion coefficients are  given by the projection of the state psi  onto the u and i basis states  so in this case of degenerate  eigenvalues the probability of measuring  eigenvalue lambda n  as given by postulate 4 needs to be  updated to the sum  over the gn dimensional degenerate  subspace  and again we can rewrite this directly  in terms of the expansion coefficients  c  what i want to discuss next is how we  can rewrite the probability of the  outcome of a measurement in terms of a  projection operator  although this is a less familiar  expression than the one we have been  discussing so far  it is very convenient in some cases for  example when discussing the density  operator  so it is definitely worth going over it  remember from the video on projection  operators that we can write the operator  that projects onto the gn dimensional  subspace of state space  spanned by the basis states u and i as  pn  and it is given by the sum of the  corresponding subspace of the outer  products of the basis states  imagine that our system is in state psi  and then construct a new state  psi n as the projection of psi onto the  gn dimensional subspace associated with  eigenvalue lambda n  writing the explicit expression for the  projection operator we get this  this here is the usual expansion  coefficient c  so we can then rewrite the state psi n  like this  let's now consider the norm of psi n  using the expression we just got here we  can rewrite it in terms of a sum over i  and then a sum over j  as usual we can move the sum over j to  the beginning and we rearrange the  expression to get this  the base is orthonormal so this is delta  ij  and we can reduce the double sum to a  single sum over i of the absolute value  squared of cni  comparing this last expression with the  one up here we see that they are the  same  so this is simply the probability of  measuring eigenvalue lambda n  so what does this mean we can rewrite  the probability p  lambda n as equal to the norm of state  psi  n this in turn is given by the  projection of state  psi onto the gn dimensional subspace  associated with eigenvalue lambda n  projection operators are hermitian so  this is equal to pn  squared projection operators are also  idempotent  so pn squared is equal to pn and overall  this means that we can rewrite the  probability of a measurement outcome  as the expectation value of the  projection operator pn  onto the subspace spanned by the  eigenstates associated with the  eigenvalue that we're measuring  as i said earlier this is an expression  that is not so common  but if you want to see it in action  check out the video on the density  operator  the final thing i want to look at is  what happens when we have a continuous  eigenvalue spectrum in this case the  eigenvalue equation  is a v alpha equal to alpha v alpha  where the eigenvalue alpha is a  continuous variable  again we can expand the state psi in the  v alpha basis  and the expansion coefficients are still  given by the projection of psi  onto the basis states  from basic probability theory when we  have a continuous variable we need to  work with probability densities rather  than  absolute probabilities we therefore look  at the probability  of getting a measurement outcome in the  range between alpha  and alpha plus d alpha we call this  probability  d p alpha and it is equal to the  absolute value squared of v  alpha psi multiplied by d alpha  this in turn can be written as usual in  terms of the expansion coefficients like  this  so what does this mean for continuous  eigenvalues the  absolute value squared of the expansion  coefficients gives the probability  density  at that eigenvalue and to obtain actual  probabilities  we must first define a range of possible  outcomes say an infinitesimal range d  alpha  and then in this case multiply that  range by the probability density  if instead of an infinitesimal range we  have a finite range  then we integrate the probability  density over that range to get the total  probability  the most famous example of a continuous  variable is perhaps position  which leads to the position  representation restricting the  discussion to one dimension  a general state psi written in the  position representation  is given by this  the expansion coefficients are still  given by the usual expression  but in this case they are typically  written with the symbol psi  rather than the symbol c of course these  are nothing more than what we call the  wave function of the system  and you can find further details about  this in the corresponding video linked  in the description  what this discussion teaches us is that  when we work in the position  representation in terms of wave  functions  the absolute value squared of the wave  function gives the probability density  of finding the quantum particle at  position x and this of course is an  elementary result that many of you will  probably recognize  measurements in quantum mechanics  introduce the famous probabilistic  nature of this theory this leads to many  strange phenomena  but i would say this is also what makes  quantum theory so exciting to study  you can find many videos in the  description and in the channel where we  explore the weird consequences of  measurements  so do check them out and if you haven't  yet seen the companion video where we  explore the conceptual understanding of  quantum measurements go take a look  i think you'll find it really  interesting and as always  if you liked the video please subscribe

